\end{aligned}$$
where $\mathbf{f}_t$ is a vector of length equal to the length of the cell state $\mathbf{c}_t$, and $\sigma$ is the sigmoid function that outputs values in the range $[0,1]$. The idea is that most values in $\mathbf{f}_t$ will be near either $1$ (remember) or 0 (forget). Later $\mathbf{f}_t$ will be pointwise multiplied by the cell state to forget the values where $\mathbf{f}_t$ is zero.

concepts.content:
Another component chooses what values to write to the cell state, and where to write them:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{i}_t &= \sigma(\mathbf{W}_i\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{U}_i\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t] + \mathbf{b}_i) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{which indices to write to}\\
    \tilde{\mathbf{c}}_t &= \texttt{tanh}(\mathbf{W}_C\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{U}_c\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t] + \mathbf{b}_c) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{what to write to those indices}
\end{aligned}$$
where $\mathbf{i}_t$ is similar to $\mathbf{f}_t$ (it is a vector whose values are nearly 0 or 1 of length equal to the length of the cell state) and it determines which indices of $\tilde{\mathbf{c}}_t$ will be written to the cell state.

